export const learningContract = Object.freeze({
  driving_question: 'How does an LLM workload turn a rack of GPUs into memory, service and power requirements?',
  fixed_boundary: 'One GB300 NVL72 is the hardware anchor. Llama 3.1 70B memory estimates, chosen service targets, production H100 evidence and original timing models are explicitly separate.',
  changed_variable: 'Context length, request membership, runtime, phase alignment and transition duration change one mechanism at a time.',
  primary_payoff: 'Translate model state and token delivery into a measured workload brief for electrical supply and cooling.',
  misconception: 'Rated FLOPS, fitted weights or average power establish delivered tokens or a suitable power supply.',
  closing_question: 'What must be measured at the intended workload and meter before choosing supply and buffering?',
});
export const initialState = Object.freeze({ schedule:'sync', relation:'independent', transitionSeconds:2 });
const brief='d02-workload-brief', productive='d02-productive-utilization', phases='d02-phases-and-envelopes';
const choice=(key,label,options)=>({key,label,options});
export const scenes = [
 {id:'workload-purpose',label:'Why the workload comes first',title:'The model and its users determine what the data center must deliver.',reference:brief,pedagogical_role:'orientation',boundary:'Chapter route: model state → token service → power over time.',explanation:['The previous chapter located power, heat and network paths. This chapter gives those systems a job. First compare learning a model with serving it. Then turn model state and request length into a memory account, explain how requests share accelerators, and inspect the resulting power trace.','The output is a workload brief for the next supply chapter: the named hardware and software, input/output lengths, traffic and response objectives, plus time-resolved power at a declared meter. These requirements cannot be recovered from accelerator count or peak FLOPS alone.']},
 {id:'success-brief',label:'One GB300 NVL72',title:'One GB300 NVL72 provides 72 GPUs; its token service still needs a definition.',reference:brief,pedagogical_role:'problem',boundary:'Named hardware: GB300 NVL72 · example model: Llama 3.1 70B · service target is chosen, not a benchmark.',explanation:['NVIDIA specifies 72 Blackwell Ultra GPUs in a GB300 NVL72 rack. Use Llama 3.1 70B as the model anchor, without claiming a measured deployment or a particular partition. A concrete example requirement is 100 simultaneous active sessions, each sustaining 40 output tokens per second after its first token. That asks for 4,000 output tokens per second in aggregate.','This is a demand calculation, not a claim that one rack meets it or needs its entire capacity for it. Also specify prompt and output lengths, time to first token, quality, precision, software and arrivals. Training the same model would instead need an experiment, data budget, quality target and deadline. A rack has no universal tokens-per-second conversion factor.']},
 {id:'model-work',label:'Training beside inference',title:'Training updates model parameters; inference spends compute to produce tokens.',reference:brief,pedagogical_role:'comparison',boundary:'Contrasting LLM uses · exact training and serving implementations vary.',explanation:['Training performs a forward pass, evaluates a loss, computes gradients and applies an optimizer update. Distributed workers may exchange gradients or other state. The objective is useful model progress by a deadline, not merely maximum GPU activity.','Inference processes a prompt and generates output tokens using configured parameters. It needs request state and working memory even though the depicted serving path does not update learned weights. Throughput, time to first token and time between tokens describe different parts of that service. The next comparison shows why those uses demand different memory.']},
 {id:'memory-comparison',label:'One model, two state budgets',title:'The same 70B model needs much more state when its parameters are trained.',reference:brief,pedagogical_role:'balance',boundary:'Rounded 70 billion parameters · decimal GB · BF16 serving weights versus a classic mixed-precision Adam training ledger.',explanation:['For inference, two bytes per parameter gives approximately 140 GB of weights. A classic mixed-precision Adam account includes 2-byte weights, 2-byte gradients, 4-byte master weights and two 4-byte optimizer moments: 16 bytes per parameter, or approximately 1,120 GB of training state.','Neither number is total runtime memory. Add request KV cache and workspace for inference, or activations and communication buffers for training. Optimizer, precision, recomputation, sharding and offload change the account. This is why a model that fits for serving can require distribution for training. It is not a universal 70B training minimum or a GB300 board allocation.']},
 {id:'kv-cache',label:'What a context stores',title:'Longer contexts retain more key and value state for every active request.',reference:brief,pedagogical_role:'mechanism',boundary:'Llama 3.1 70B geometry · BF16 KV · no prefix sharing, cache quantization or block overhead.',explanation:['During inference, the attention implementation can retain key and value vectors for prior tokens so they are available to subsequent decode steps. Meta’s configuration gives 80 layers, eight KV heads and head dimension 128. At two bytes per element, each cached token needs 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 bytes, or 320 KiB.','This is full-context cache accounting for this particular model and precision, not a universal bytes-per-token constant. Prompt and generated tokens both occupy context. Cache sharing, quantization, sliding windows, model structure and allocation overhead can change memory use.']},
 {id:'context-capacity',label:'Context changes concurrency',title:'A fourfold context increase cuts this cache pool from 25 requests to six.',reference:brief,pedagogical_role:'comparison',boundary:'Chosen 64 GiB KV pool · 320 KiB per cached token · equal full contexts · capacity estimate, not throughput.',explanation:['At 8,192 cached tokens, one request uses 2.5 GiB. At 32,768 it uses 10 GiB. Floor division of the chosen 64 GiB cache pool gives 25 or six complete requests. The 64 GiB is an explicit allocation decision, not all memory on a named GPU. Weights and other runtime state occupy separate allocations.','Keeping 100 sessions resident at those full lengths would require at least four or seventeen such independent cache pools under this arithmetic model. That is not a GPU count: replication, tensor parallelism and implementation determine which devices own a pool. The connection to facility design is context → resident concurrency → serving replicas and their measured power. Memory fit alone does not establish token speed.']},
 {id:'prefill-decode',label:'Prompt processing and generation',title:'Serving a prompt and streaming its answer put different demands on the GPUs.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'mechanism',boundary:'Autoregressive LLM serving · simplified prefill and decode · no latency or token-rate prediction.',explanation:['Prefill processes the prompt and creates the initial request state. Decode repeatedly extends the sequence, commonly producing one new token per active request per iteration. Each new token depends on previous context; batching multiple requests creates more work to perform together.','Time to first token includes queueing, prompt processing and first-token work. Inter-token latency concerns the stream after that point. Prefill often offers more parallel matrix work; low-batch decode can be constrained by memory bandwidth. Neither phase has a universal power level, and speculative decoding or other implementations change execution. Peak FLOPS alone is therefore insufficient to predict delivered token throughput.']},
 {id:'continuous-batching',label:'Continuous batching',title:'Continuous batching admits a new request when another leaves the active batch.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'mechanism',boundary:'Two illustrative decode slots · A needs 2 steps, B needs 5, C needs 3 · C already waiting · prefill costs omitted.',explanation:['A fixed batch can leave a slot unused after one sequence finishes while a longer sequence continues. In this illustration, A finishes after two decode steps. Continuous batching admits queued request C into that slot while B continues, instead of waiting for B’s whole response. vLLM documents iteration-level request scheduling and continuous batching.','This is a scheduler mechanism, not a measured speedup. The numbered cells are decode iterations, not equal wall-clock durations guaranteed under both policies. Prefill, KV capacity, token budgets and implementation determine which new work can be admitted. It matters here because request membership changes active compute and memory; continuous batching does not guarantee a flat electrical load.']},
 {id:'energy-per-result',label:'Compare the same completed work',title:'A lower-power run can use more energy to finish the same token workload.',reference:productive,pedagogical_role:'comparison',boundary:'Original relative comparison · same accepted outputs and quality · complete-run mean power · no product benchmark.',explanation:['Hold completed useful work fixed. Run B has 80 percent of A’s mean power but takes 150 percent of its duration. Its energy is 0.8 × 1.5 = 1.2 times A’s, so the slower run consumes 20 percent more energy for the same accepted token workload. The comparison is shown simultaneously.','At 80 percent power, break-even duration is 1/0.8 = 1.25 times as long. Whether a real power cap, model configuration or serving policy crosses that boundary requires a complete benchmark. Include waiting and supporting equipment within the same meter boundary; a sample of the compute phase cannot substitute for whole-run energy.']},
 {id:'resource-paths',label:'Locate the stalled training step',title:'A training step can stop making progress while its GPUs wait for an exchange.',reference:productive,pedagogical_role:'diagnosis',boundary:'Illustrative synchronous training dependency · no measured delay or power magnitude.',explanation:['A data-parallel training iteration may require gradient synchronization before an update can complete. If a required exchange is late, the next dependent work waits even while the workers remain allocated and powered. The visual connects the delay to a specific dependency, rather than equating allocation with useful output.','Correlate GPU activity and power with collective timings, network counters and storage events. That observation distinguishes an exposed communication wait from input starvation or checkpoint work; it does not prove a root cause from one utilization number. Communication and compute can overlap in real implementations. The following production trace shows why synchronized work also matters to electrical supply.']},
 {id:'training-power-evidence',label:'A measured training trace',title:'Production training racks show repeated power swings as their work synchronizes.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'evidence',boundary:'DGX-H100 production training trace · Choukse et al., 2025, Figure 1 · normalized power, not GB300 wattage.',explanation:['The Microsoft, OpenAI and NVIDIA authors publish power readings from an at-scale training job on DGX-H100 racks. Their discussion connects high compute activity and lower-power communication/checkpoint phases to synchronized power variation. This source figure establishes that the concern is observed in practice.','The figure is normalized and does not supply a GB300 power specification. Memory access is not inherently maximum-power work, and lower arithmetic activity does not mean the network or GPUs use zero power. Later figures evaluating storage in this paper are simulations; they are not being presented as production storage measurements.']},
 {id:'job-phases',label:'Explain one training rhythm',title:'Compute and exchange phases create a time-varying electrical load.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'mechanism',boundary:'Original teaching trace · 30 s at 120 kW compute, 15 s at 40 kW exchange, 15 s at 60 kW save · not the H100 measurements.',explanation:['This simplified training cycle isolates three activities: compute, an exposed exchange interval and checkpoint save. Its powers and durations are declared assumptions chosen for legible arithmetic, not a digitization of the previous production trace. The phases explain the shape that electrical equipment sees.','Over 60 seconds, energy is 120 × 30 + 40 × 15 + 60 × 15 = 5,100 kJ, giving 85 kW mean. The 120 kW plateau and each transition remain separate requirements. Data centers need the power trace at the relevant shared electrical boundary, with enough resolution to retain those events.']},
 {id:'synchronized-jobs',label:'Sum coincident activity',title:'When four groups enter compute together, their shared supply sees a 480 kW peak.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'mechanism',boundary:'Four copies of the original 60 s trace · shared meter · simultaneous phases · workload independence not yet assumed.',explanation:['Sum four identical power traces at the same timestamps: 480 kW during compute, 160 during exchange and 240 during save. Their shared mean is 340 kW. This is the link between distributed workload timing and the upstream supply that serves the groups.','Coincident groups could be independent jobs or participants in a coupled training job. That distinction does not change the sum, but it changes whether moving their start times is a legitimate remedy. The next two slides make that condition explicit.']},
 {id:'staggering-jobs',label:'A conditional scheduling example',title:'Independent jobs can lower a coincident peak if shifting them preserves their service.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'comparison',boundary:'Conditional model · four independent periodic jobs · no contention, extra waiting or missed deadline · not a deployment claim.',controls:[choice('schedule','Phase offsets',[['sync','All start together'],['staggered','Offset by 15 s']])],explanation:['Under these explicit independence assumptions, offset four copies by 0, 15, 30 and 45 seconds. At every point in the periodic cycle, two compute, one exchanges and one saves: 340 kW. Each job retains the same phase durations, so the 5.667 kWh cycle energy is preserved.','This is a counterfactual for reasoning, not evidence that production AI clusters routinely stagger jobs this way. Startup, completion, traffic deadlines, shared-resource contention and failures are excluded. The calculation is useful only when the next dependency check passes.']},
 {id:'independence',label:'The dependency determines the remedy',title:'Delaying one worker in a coupled training job can make the other workers wait.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'counterexample',boundary:'Dependency illustration · no predicted energy saving for the coupled case.',controls:[choice('relation','Relationship',[['independent','Separate jobs'],['coupled','Workers in one job']])],explanation:['Independent jobs have their own progress requirements; a delay can be considered if deadlines and shared resources permit. Workers in one synchronous training job may instead have to reach the same exchange before dependent computation can proceed. Delaying one can lengthen the others’ wait.','That breaks the no-extra-waiting assumption behind the flat staggering trace. An inference request load balancer can distribute eligible requests among replicas; it cannot arbitrarily desynchronize collective operations inside one distributed training job. The right remedy depends on the dependency graph.']},
 {id:'demand-transition',label:'Read the power trace',title:'Use power over time to specify both peak demand and the required response speed.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'design',boundary:'Original shared-load example · 480 → 160 kW transition · separate assumed transition duration.',controls:[choice('transitionSeconds','Transition duration',[[2,'2 seconds'],[0.2,'0.2 seconds']])],explanation:['The power-versus-time graph carries three distinct pieces of information: energy under the trace, peak power and how quickly the load changes. The 340 kW full-cycle average does not specify a supply capable of the 480 kW plateau or the response needed for a 320 kW transition.','For the isolated transition, 2 seconds gives a 160 kW/s magnitude; 0.2 seconds gives 1,600 kW/s. These are assumed transition measurements, separate from the ideal cycle used for its mean. Measure at the intended boundary and sample rapidly enough for the controller or storage question. Local buffering, upstream controls and sustained source capacity solve different parts of the problem.']},
 {id:'power-response',label:'Match the response to the cause',title:'Scheduling, GPU power controls and local storage change different parts of the power trace.',reference:phases,pedagogical_role:'comparison',boundary:'Mechanisms discussed by Choukse et al., 2025 · controls and storage require platform-specific qualification.',explanation:['A scheduler changes when eligible work runs, subject to dependencies and deadlines. GPU power limits or supported smoothing controls change device behavior and may affect runtime or energy. A local buffer absorbs or supplies the difference between source power and load power, within charge, discharge, energy and thermal limits.','The 2025 paper presents production workload evidence, hardware power-smoothing microbenchmarks and simulated storage studies. Those are distinct evidence types. A request load balancer is not an electrical buffer; a BBU cannot cover an ongoing average-power deficit indefinitely; and continuous batching is not a promise of smooth power. Later power lessons examine the converter and storage response in detail.']},
 {id:'next-brief',label:'Carry the workload into supply design',title:'Take a defined token service and its measured power trace into the supply design.',reference:brief,pedagogical_role:'handoff',boundary:'A design brief, not a pass/fail quiz · service target chosen; measured rack performance remains to be established.',explanation:['Carry the named hardware, model and software; prompt/output lengths, simultaneous sessions, output-token target, time-to-first-token target and quality conditions. Then benchmark that workload and record complete-run energy alongside peak demand, transition speed and recovery behavior at the relevant IT or facility meter.','The next chapter can use those requirements to reason about deliverable supply, redundancy and buffering. What remains unknown must remain visible. A 72-GPU rack, a memory-fit calculation or a nameplate power alone does not establish a service that produces 4,000 usable output tokens per second.']},
];
